,429,15448,304,279,11031,13,1205,646,990,279,4621,504,279,1459,1124,1188,16,11,220,15,11,220,16,10699,8,311,2441,1459,389,279,1555,11,1977,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,85,25547,16,8,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,17,24984,220,18,24984,220,15,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,568,576,4621,374,1124,11520,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,17,24984,220,18,24984,220,15,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,481,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,15,24984,220,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,18,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe line parameterized by\n\\[\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\]lies in a plane of the form $x + y + cz = d.$  Enter the ordered pair $(c,d).$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo determine the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) for the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\) that contains the line parameterized by \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the normal vector to the plane and a point on the plane.\n\nFirst, identify a point on the line. When \\(t = 0\\), the point is \\(\\mathbf{v}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). So, the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) lies on the plane.\n\nNext, find the direction vector of the line. The direction vector is given by the coefficients of \\(t\\) in the parameterized equation, which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe normal vector to the plane can be found by taking the cross product of the direction vector of the line and any vector that lies in the plane. We can use the vector from the point \\((1, 0, 1)\\) to another point on the line, say \\(\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\). The vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d57334410a120cd27f5eb65d319b4d31d5293b0f22bd1a5b219e393a4aa9634e"},"seconds":18.629504680633545,"seed":144858483316681177,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) for the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\) that contains the line parameterized by \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to determine the values of \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) such that the plane equation holds for every point on the line.\n\nFirst, substitute the parameterized coordinates of the line into the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\):\n\\[\n(1 + t) + (3t) + c(1 - t) = d.\n\\]\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n1 + t + 3t + c - ct = d \\implies 1 + c + (4 - c)t = d.\n\\]\nSince this equation must hold for all values of \\(t\\), the coefficient of \\(t\\) must be zero, and the constant term must equal \\(d\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n4 - c = 0 \\implies c = 4,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n1 + c = d \\implies 1 + 4 = d \\implies d = 5.\n\\]\nThus, the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(4, 5)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(4, 5)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) for the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\) that contains the line parameterized by \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 - t \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to determine the values of \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) such that the plane equation holds for every point on the line.\n\nFirst, substitute the parameterized coordinates of the line into the plane equation \\(x + y + cz = d\\):\n\\[\n(1 + t) + (3t) + c(1 - t) = d.\n\\]\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n1 + t + 3t + c - ct = d \\implies 1 + c + (4 - c)t = d.\n\\]\nSince this equation must hold for all values of \\(t\\), the coefficient of \\(t\\) must be zero, and the constant term must equal \\(d\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n4 - c = 0 \\implies c = 4,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n1 + c = d \\implies 1 + 4 = d \\implies d = 5.\n\\]\nThus, the ordered pair \\((c, d)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(4, 5)}\\).","id":"precalculus:264"}
